- assemble_transport_leg: requires origin_availability_locked, destination_availability_locked, origin_availability_node_linked, destination_availability_node_linked, origin_node_ready, destination_node_ready, origin_availability_confirmed, destination_availability_confirmed, transport_leg_available. Effect: transport_leg_assembled, transport_leg_origin_linked, transport_leg_destination_linked, not transport_leg_available.

Goal requires origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready both true, destination_node_occupied and destination_node_ready both true, origin_availability_confirmed and locked, destination_availability_confirmed and locked. So we need both nodes occupied and ready, both availabilities confirmed and locked. None of the assemble actions have both origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready as preconditions; they have either occupied or ready, not both. However we can achieve both by loading and unloading payload as earlier described, which will give both occupied and ready after load then unload.

Similarly for availabilities: we need both confirmed and locked; we can achieve by load then unload (load gives locked, unload gives confirmed). So after load then unload, we will have both locked and confirmed, and node occupied (from load) and ready (from unload). Good.

Thus we need to load origin payload, then unload origin payload; load destination payload, then unload destination payload. After these sequences, we will have origin_node_occupied, origin_node_ready, origin_availability_locked, origin_availability_confirmed all true. Same for destination.

Now which assemble action to use? After we have both nodes occupied and ready, and both availabilities confirmed and locked, we need an assemble action that matches those preconditions. Let's see:

- assemble_fully_loaded_leg requires origin_node_occupied, destination_node_occupied, but not origin_availability_confirmed nor destination_availability_confirmed (requires them NOT confirmed). So not suitable.

- assemble_destination_loaded_leg requires origin_node_ready, destination_node_occupied, origin_availability_confirmed, not destination_availability_confirmed.

- assemble_origin_loaded_leg requires origin_node_occupied, destination_node_ready, not origin_availability_confirmed, destination_availability_confirmed.

- assemble_transport_leg requires origin_node_ready, destination_node_ready, origin_availability_confirmed, destination_availability_confirmed.

Thus none match both nodes occupied and ready simultaneously. However we could use assemble_transport_leg which requires origin_node_ready and destination_node_ready (not occupied). But after load then unload, we have both occupied and ready; we still have ready true (since unload adds ready, does not remove occupied). So origin_node_ready is true (since we added it via unload). Similarly destination_node_ready true. So we have origin_node_ready true, destination_node_ready true. Also we have origin_availability_confirmed true, destination_availability_confirmed true. Also we have origin_availability_locked true, destination_availability_locked true (from load). So assemble_transport_leg preconditions: origin_availability_locked, destination_availability_locked, origin_availability_node_linked, destination_availability_node_linked, origin_node_ready, destination_node_ready, origin_availability_confirmed, destination_availability_confirmed, transport_leg_available. All these we will have after load/unload sequences. Good! So we can use assemble_transport_leg.

Thus we need to perform load and unload for origin and destination availabilities.

Now we also need transport_leg_loading_authorized true. Action authorize_transport_leg_loading ?transport_leg - transport_leg ?origin_availability - origin_candidate_availability ?route_task_profile - route_task_profile. Precondition: transport_leg_assembled ?transport_leg, origin_availability_locked ?origin_availability, candidate_task_profile_linked ?origin_availability ?route_task_profile, not transport_leg_loading_authorized ?transport_leg. Effect: transport_leg_loading_authorized ?transport_leg.

